  "cfg": {
    "locomotion": {
      "mode": "static",
      "allowedRadius": 0,
      "turnMode": "snap"
    },
    "outline": {
      "version": 1,
      "course": {
        "title": "Gears and More Gears: Building Drive Trains",
        "goal": "Learners assemble gear sets, predict rotation direction, and calculate simple and compound gear ratios to design drive trains that meet speed and direction targets.",
        "paceNote": "Learning outline generated from the knowledge graph"
      },
      "progress": {
        "completedSectionIds": []
      },
      "chapters": [
        {
          "id": "ch_msgs92h9v0d6",
          "title": "Meshing Gears and Direction",
          "summary": "Learners meet gears, see how they mesh, and discover the direction rule.",
          "sections": [
            {
              "id": "sec_msgs92h9fzwa",
              "title": "What Is a Gear?",
              "type": "reading",
              "summary": "Opening activity intro plus 'Some common types of gears' section and gear-pair photo.",
              "purpose": "Introduce gears, meshing, driver vs driven, and gear types.",
              "vr": {},
              "reading": {
                "chunks": [
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgs9kgq402l",
                    "title": "What Is a Gear?",
                    "html": "<p>A <strong>gear</strong> is a toothed wheel that transmits rotational motion. When it spins, its teeth push on whatever they touch, passing the turning motion along.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "In your own words, what makes a gear different from a plain wheel?",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "A gear has teeth around its edge that let it grip and transmit rotation to another gear.",
                      "explanation": "The teeth are the key feature — they let gears drive each other instead of just spinning freely."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgs9kgqqv9t",
                    "title": "Meshing",
                    "html": "<p>Two gears <strong>mesh</strong> when their teeth interlock. Once meshed, turning one gear forces the other to turn too — the teeth push against each other.</p><figure class=\"ws-pedagogy-fig\"><img class=\"ws-inline-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Photo of a yellow spur gear meshing with a larger green gear, each mounted on colored plastic axle rods.\"><figcaption>Photo of a yellow spur gear meshing with a larger green gear, each mounted on colored plastic axle rods.</figcaption></figure>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "mcq",
                      "question": "What must happen for two gears to mesh?",
                      "options": [
                        "Their teeth must interlock",
                        "They must be the same size",
                        "They must spin the same way",
                        "They must share one axle"
                      ],
                      "answer": "Their teeth must interlock",
                      "explanation": "Meshing means the teeth fit into each other so one gear drives the other."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgs9kgqkels",
                    "title": "Driver and Driven",
                    "html": "<p>In a meshed pair, the <strong>driver</strong> is the gear you turn — it is the input. The <strong>driven</strong> gear is the one that gets turned — it is the output.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "A motor spins gear A, which turns gear B. Which is the driver and which is the driven?",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "Gear A is the driver (input); gear B is the driven (output).",
                      "explanation": "The driver receives the input motion; the driven gear is what that motion turns."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgs9kgqz74h",
                    "title": "Meshed Gears Spin Opposite Ways",
                    "html": "<p>Here's a common mistake: people think meshed gears turn the <em>same</em> direction. They don't. Because the teeth push against each other at the contact point, if the driver turns clockwise, the driven gear turns <strong>counter‑clockwise</strong>. Every mesh flips the direction.</p><figure class=\"ws-pedagogy-fig\"><img class=\"ws-inline-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Photo of several gray gears mounted with paper-brad axles on a yellow craft-stick backing, forming a gear train.\"><figcaption>Photo of several gray gears mounted with paper-brad axles on a yellow craft-stick backing, forming a gear train.</figcaption></figure>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "mcq",
                      "question": "A driver gear spins clockwise. Which way does the gear it meshes with spin?",
                      "options": [
                        "Counter-clockwise",
                        "Clockwise",
                        "It doesn't spin",
                        "Same speed, no direction"
                      ],
                      "answer": "Counter-clockwise",
                      "explanation": "At a mesh the teeth push against each other, so the second gear always turns the opposite way."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgs9kgq0029",
                    "title": "Each Extra Gear Flips Direction Again",
                    "html": "<p>Add a third gear meshed to the second, and the direction flips <em>again</em>. So the rule is simple: <strong>count the meshes</strong>. An odd number of flips reverses direction; an even number keeps the original direction.</p>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "You have a chain of four meshed gears. The first turns clockwise. Which way does the fourth turn, and why?",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "Counter-clockwise — there are 3 meshes (an odd number of flips), so the direction reverses.",
                      "explanation": "Count meshes: gear1→2, 2→3, 3→4 = 3 flips. Odd flips reverse direction, so the last gear turns opposite the first."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgs9kgqciy6",
                    "title": "Types of Gears: Spur and Pinion",
                    "html": "<figure class=\"ws-pedagogy-fig\"><img class=\"ws-inline-img\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"Photo of a motor driving a black gear meshing into a compound stack of green, yellow, red, and blue gears on shared axles.\"><figcaption>Photo of a motor driving a black gear meshing into a compound stack of green, yellow, red, and blue gears on shared axles.</figcaption></figure><ul><li><strong>Spur gear</strong> — the most common type. Different sizes are used together to change speed or direction.</li><li><strong>Pinion gear</strong> — usually a small gear used as a driver, often attached directly to a motor.</li></ul>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "short",
                      "question": "In your own words, what is the key idea of “Types of Gears: Spur and Pinion”?",
                      "options": [
                        "",
                        "",
                        "",
                        ""
                      ],
                      "answer": "Meshing",
                      "explanation": "Name the concept and one distinguishing detail."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "chk_msgs9kgq560w",
                    "title": "Types of Gears: Rack, Worm, Bevel",
                    "html": "<ul><li><strong>Rack gear</strong> — a flat toothed bar; converts rotational motion into straight-line (linear) motion, or the reverse.</li><li><strong>Worm gear</strong> — a spiral tooth good for big reductions; it can turn the driven gear, but the driven gear can't turn it back (a locking quality).</li><li><strong>Bevel gear</strong> — changes the direction of the rotating shaft, usually at 90°.</li></ul>",
                    "followUp": {
                      "enabled": true,
                      "type": "mcq",
                      "question": "Which gear type is best for changing the axis of rotation by 90 degrees?",
                      "options": [
                        "Bevel gear",
                        "Rack gear",
                        "Spur gear",
                        "Pinion gear"
                      ],
                      "answer": "Bevel gear",
                      "explanation": "Bevel gears are made to redirect rotating shafts, usually at a 90° angle."
                    },
                    "imagePrompt": ""
                  }
                ]
              },
              "h5": {
                "prompt": "",
                "html": "",
                "status": "idle",
                "followUp": null
              },
              "quiz": {
                "items": []
              },
              "covers": [
                "n1",
